{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-18-100a-fall-2012", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["18.100A"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Introduction to Analysis", "course_slug": "18-100a-introduction-to-analysis-fall-2012", "term": "Fall", "year": 2012, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2012", "language": "en", "departments": ["Mathematics"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Arthur Mattuck"], "subject_primary": "Calculus", "subject_secondary": ["Differential Equations", "Mathematical Analysis", "Mathematical Logic"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Mathematics", "subtopics": [{"name": "Calculus", "specialties": []}, {"name": "Differential Equations", "specialties": []}, {"name": "Mathematical Analysis", "specialties": []}, {"name": "Mathematical Logic", "specialties": []}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Problem Sets"], "description": "Analysis I (18.100) in its various versions covers fundamentals of mathematical analysis: continuity, differentiability, some form of the Riemann integral, sequences and series of numbers and functions, uniform convergence with applications to interchange of limit operations, some point-set topology, including some work in Euclidean n-space. MIT students may choose to take one of three versions of 18.100: Option A (18.100A) chooses less abstract definitions and proofs, and gives applications where possible. Option B (18.100B) is more demanding and for students with more mathematical maturity; it places more emphasis from the beginning on point-set topology and n-space, whereas Option A is concerned primarily with analysis on the real line, saving for the last weeks work in 2-space (the plane) and its point-set topology. Option C (18.100C) is a 15-unit variant of Option B, with further instruction and practice in written and oral communication.", "course_url": "h